# What EAS Build does NOT need. The archive was 230 MB, which made both the fingerprint step and
# the upload slow enough that the CLI warned about each. None of the below is a build input — the
# app bundle is src/ + assets/ + the native dirs.
#
# Careful: .easignore REPLACES .gitignore for the archive, so anything gitignored that must stay
# out has to be repeated here. node_modules and dist are the ones that matter.
node_modules/
dist/
.expo/

# Design + docs — 94 mock HTML files, specs, punchlists, handoffs.
design-mocks/
*.md
!README.md

# The separate admin dashboard and marketing site: their own apps, not part of this bundle.
admin/
site/

# Database migrations and edge functions ship via `supabase db push` / `functions deploy`,
# never inside the app binary.
supabase/

# Agent worktrees. `.claude/worktrees/` holds a FULL checkout per parallel agent — ten of them
# during wave-0, ~61 MB each — and none of it is a build input. Left out, the archive had grown
# from the 230 MB this file was written to fix, back up to 506 MB: about 80% of every upload was
# ten copies of the same repo. This is also why the fingerprint step started warning that it was
# taking longer than expected.
.claude/

# Local tooling and one-off artifacts.
scripts/
*.ics
*.png.bak

# The generated native dirs. Gitignored, but .easignore REPLACES .gitignore for the archive, so
# they need saying again here — and this one is not just size, it is correctness: with android/
# present in the upload EAS reads the project as BARE and SKIPS PREBUILD, so it would build
# whatever native config happened to be on the machine that last ran `expo prebuild` rather than
# what app.config.ts says. That is exactly how a FOCUS_NUDGE_ANDROID=1 verification prebuild would
# leak the AccessibilityService into a build that was meant to ship without it.
/android
/ios
